diff --git a/docs/rspec-performance.md b/docs/rspec-performance.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5480fd7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/rspec-performance.md
@@ -0,0 +1,53 @@
+# Serial RSpec profiling: models
+
+Run from the repository root, with the normal test database and Redis running:
+
+```sh
+bundle exec rspec spec/models --seed 1234 --profile 15
+```
+
+Compare RSpec's `Finished in` time, which excludes file loading. Keep the seed,
+filters, services, and coverage settings the same for comparisons; use another
+seed to check isolation. The numbers below are local reference points (Ruby
+4.0.6, PostgreSQL 17, no coverage or parallelism), not CI time budgets.
+
+| Model run | Seconds | Examples | Failures |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Baseline | 170.2 | 1,795 | 0 |
+| After model setup changes | 53.19 | 1,795 | 0 |
+
+That is a 68.7% reduction (3.20× speedup) at seed 1234.
+A second order (seed 4321) passed all 1,795 examples in 53.73s.
+
+## What cost time
+
+- Only 14% of the baseline was in the slowest 15 examples. Look for work that
+  happens *during setup*: `log/test.log` showed `TriggerWebhookWorker#perform`
+  loading records and serializing webhook payloads even for unrelated model
+  assertions. Model examples now enqueue Sidekiq jobs in fake mode. Use
+  `sidekiq: :inline` on the narrowest group or example that needs a job's
+  result. That includes negative assertions about delivery: an empty mailbox
+  isn't evidence of correct behavior if delivery jobs never ran.
+- `Status::FetchRepliesConcern` enabled `ActiveRecord.verbose_query_logs` at
+  file load. This added a call-site lookup to **every** logged query in a run
+  containing that spec. Don't leave process-wide debugging flags in specs;
+  ordinary SQL logging is still available.
+- Baseline group totals included 16.2s for `AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy`,
+  12.9s for `TagFeed`, 9.0s for `PublicFeed`, and 6.7s for
+  `Status::ThreadingConcern`. These repeatedly built the same graphs of
+  accounts, statuses and associations. Use TestProf's `let_it_be` / `before_all`
+  for stable rows within a transactional group. Keep changing inputs (e.g. the
+  cleanup policy or a viewer) example-local. Use `refind: true` when mutating
+  records or their associations: rolling back SQL doesn't reset Ruby objects.
+  Don't share Redis state, job queues, or files that examples modify this way.
+
+## Next measurement
+
+After these changes, `MediaAttachment` was the slowest file (6.4s): audio
+cover art and thumbnail specs really do need file processing. Keep those
+checks intact rather than disabling attachment processing suite-wide.
+
+Profile `spec/requests` separately before changing it. Do not assume that fake
+jobs are appropriate there: a request can check downstream effects across
+models. Add the two separately measured serial times to track the combined
+speedup. This phase did not benchmark requests or change fabricators.
